<p>Mexican security forces killed Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes, known as “El Mencho,” the longtime leader of the Jalisco New Generation Cartel (CJNG), in a weekend operation in Jalisco state. Officials said intelligence work and surveillance helped track him through a romantic partner. Authorities said the takedown quickly set off coordinated retaliation: cartel-linked gunmen erected roadblocks, torched vehicles, and attacked businesses. In some areas, officials urged people to stay indoors, and daily life was disrupted in places including Guadalajara and tourist hubs like Puerto Vallarta. By Monday, the government said it was working to steady the situation—clearing blockades, sending major reinforcements including about 10,000 troops, and continuing arrests. The backlash was costly: Mexico’s security secretary said at least 25 National Guard members were killed in Jalisco in separate attacks after the operation, along with deaths among suspected cartel members and other fatalities reported by officials and news outlets. Even with “El Mencho” gone, several outlets said CJNG’s next chapter is unclear—its leadership could splinter or regroup—leaving residents and businesses bracing for more reprisals as authorities try to stabilize the region. Still, officials said key roads had reopened and blockades were lifted, a modest but real sign of relief after a frightening 24 to 48 hours for many communities.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Xo1c-yLlC8yUrSeElRTJc2sTpyJeztOGZWUHwIQnayfSojiFmU4c_hkZH3SK13BSjx_fwsGH8Wi1VWjFs-kwZa_9YFf3Sv7jOii3tyEpJA_c-6-Ta9ymzJ62wbRFM6E_R5KM-nDDeH2r9Bx00jUuC32FXHr2nxy4gHIKAuDxtzk0j9H1AP25Xi9jtcEDk4H--9bsJbYeWqMWwFxIO-Grk1bBkPsol6n2s37zr7xr' alt='Mexican special forces in Mexico City after the operation targeting CJNG leader Nemesio Oseguera Cervantes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>How he was found: Officials said the raid located “El Mencho” at a cabin hideout near Tapalpa in Jalisco, and that CIA-provided U.S. intelligence was “critical” in narrowing down his location.</li><li>Blockades count: Mexico’s security secretary said cartel-linked groups created 252 road obstructions nationwide, including 85 in Jalisco, before authorities cleared them.</li><li>Geographic spread: Officials said the worst-hit states included Jalisco, as well as Baja California, the State of Mexico, Michoacán, Guanajuato, Guerrero, Veracruz, Sinaloa and Tamaulipas.</li><li>Policy signal: An El País commentary argued the killing signaled a shift away from “hugs, not bullets” toward a more forceful anti-cartel approach—one that analysts note can trigger swift retaliation.</li><li>Cross-border pressure: Several analyses said the operation underscored deepening U.S.-Mexico security cooperation and came amid pressure from President Trump to step up action against drug trafficking.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mexico security secretary Omar García Harfuch: He said the country’s narco-blockades had been lifted and reported 70 arrests across seven states after the CJNG backlash. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-02-23/narco-blockades-lifted-in-mexico-after-cartel-reaction-to-death-of-el-mencho.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>President Claudia Sheinbaum: She said calm was being restored and authorities were removing improvised cartel roadblocks after the operation. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/2/23/mexican-officials-say-25-soldiers-killed-in-clashes-following-cartel-raid'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Mexico’s defense leadership: Defense Minister Ricardo Trevilla Trejo offered condolences to families of soldiers killed and said troops “fulfilled their mission,” according to reporting from Mexico City. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/23/mexico-trump-cartel-boss-killing'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Christian Science Monitor analysis: It argued that taking down a cartel leader can be a clear win for the state, but the swift retaliatory violence is a reminder that peace may not arrive quickly. (<a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Americas/2026/0223/el-mencho-jalisco-drug-violence-mexico'>The Christian Science Monitor</a>)</li><li>WSWS perspective: It described the killing as a U.S.-backed “execution” and warned it could be used to deepen militarization and U.S. influence in Mexico. (<a href='https://www.wsws.org/en/articles/2026/02/24/mwzd-f24.html'>World Socialist Web Site</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/02/23/world/mexico'>Violence Ebbs but Mexico Remains on Edge After Killing of Cartel Boss</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-02-23/the-final-hours-of-el-mencho-the-worlds-most-wanted-criminal.html'>The final hours of El Mencho, the world’s most wanted criminal</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/mexico-fears-violence-after-army-kills-leader-powerful-130400128'>25 Mexican National Guard troops left dead in operation that killed cartel leader</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Americas/2026/0223/el-mencho-jalisco-drug-violence-mexico'>Mexico takes out most-wanted cartel leader: Why that means nationwide upheaval</a> - csmonitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/mexican-jalisco-new-generation-cartel-leader-el-mencho-spent-night-with-girlfriend-before-raid-mexico-was-tracking-her-11127801#publisher=newsstand'>How El Mencho&#x27;s Girlfriend Was Key In Taking Down The Drug Lord</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1rcp8s0/mexicos_mostwanted_cartel_leader_was_killed_after/'>Mexico&#x27;s most-wanted cartel leader was killed after visit from romantic partner</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/02/24/mexico-deploys-10-000-troops-to-restore-order-after-violence-linked-to-the-death-of-drug-lord-el-mencho_6750815_4.html'>Mexico deploys 10,000 troops to restore order af